# The pinned UPSTREAM RELEASE ASSETS for .tooling/bin, in sha256sum(1) format, with paths
# relative to the download directory .tooling/downloads. Check with:
#   ( cd .tooling/downloads && sha256sum -c ../../crooks-assistant/docs/dev-environment/binary-assets.sha256 )
#
# This is the gate that runs BEFORE anything is extracted. binaries.sha256 checks what ended up
# in .tooling/bin; this file checks what came off the network, so a substituted download is
# rejected while it is still an inert file. Both are enforced and either mismatch stops the plan.
#
# Provenance of these digests, recorded 2026-09-19 during the ENV-REPRO-001 continuation:
#   - Each asset was fetched once from the asset_url pinned in manifest.json and digested here.
#   - Extracting each asset reproduced the corresponding digest in binaries.sha256 EXACTLY, for
#     all nine installed binaries. binaries.sha256 was recorded by observing the builder at
#     9a27bc441adad1e98e8a9ca257d1883246ee7eec, so the observation and the upstream release now
#     agree: what is installed on the builder is what these releases contain.
#   - Where the project publishes its own checksum file (manifest `upstream_checksums`), the
#     digest below was also compared with it and matched: shfmt, gitleaks, trivy, uv.
#   - shellcheck, fd, ast-grep and hyperfine publish no checksum file. Their digests are
#     first-fetch observations, enforced here but NOT upstream-attested, and doctor prints one
#     advisory line naming exactly those four rather than leaving the gap unsaid.

